prompt:
You are a financial analyst revising a forecast based on a peer critique.

Original forecast: {
  "strategy": "baseline",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "forecast": [
    {
      "year": 2023,
      "revenue_cr": 3944.14,
      "net_income_cr": 211.19,
      "eps": 143.56
    },
    {
      "year": 2024,
      "revenue_cr": 5914.53,
      "net_income_cr": 316.61,
      "eps": 215.22
    },
    {
      "year": 2025,
      "revenue_cr": 8869.55,
      "net_income_cr": 474.67,
      "eps": 322.67
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "From the standalone P&L, Revenue from Operations was \u20b92,631.13 Cr in FY2022 vs \u20b91,754.40 Cr in FY2021, implying a YoY revenue growth rate of ~49.97% (~50%). Net Income (PAT) was \u20b9140.96 Cr in FY2022 vs \u20b9-34.61 Cr in FY2021; since FY2021 was a loss year, the FY2022 net profit margin of ~5.36% is applied to projected revenues to derive net income (i.e., net income grows in line with revenue at ~50% annually). Shares outstanding remain at 1,47,10,764 (unchanged per the equity statement), used as the denominator for EPS calculation."
}
Critique: {
  "company": "POWERMECH",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "strategy": "baseline",
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Revenue will continue to grow at ~50% per annum for three consecutive years, extrapolated directly from a single-year FY2021-to-FY2022 growth rate",
      "failure_scenario": "The FY2022 ~50% revenue surge was likely driven by a post-COVID recovery bounce and project order bunching rather than structural capacity expansion; as the base effect normalises, revenue growth reverts to the industry mean of 12-18%, causing FY2025 actual revenue to be less than half the forecast figure",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Net profit margin remains fixed at 5.36% (FY2022 level) throughout the forecast period, with net income growing in strict lockstep with revenue",
      "failure_scenario": "Rising input costs (steel, fuel, subcontractor labour), intensifying competitive bidding pressure in the EPC/O&M segment, and operating leverage reversal on a slower revenue base compress margins to 3-4%, causing net income to fall well below forecast even if revenue targets are partially met",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Shares outstanding remain constant at 1,47,10,764 throughout the forecast period with zero dilution",
      "failure_scenario": "To fund the working-capital requirements implied by a tripling of revenue, the company undertakes a QIP or rights issue, or exercises ESOPs, expanding the share count by 10-15% and reducing EPS materially below the forecast figures even if revenue and net income targets are achieved",
      "risk": "Medium"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 18,
  "overall_critique": "This forecast mechanically compounds a one-off post-COVID revenue recovery rate for three years without any mean-reversion adjustment, order-book validation, or capacity constraint analysis, making the implied FY2025 revenue of ~\u20b98,870 Cr almost certainly a significant overestimate. Anchoring margin assumptions to a single recovery-year profit margin while ignoring cost inflation, competitive dynamics, and balance-sheet stress from hyper-growth further compounds the forecast error, rendering the EPS projections largely unreliable as a valuation input."
}

Step 1: For each "High" risk assumption in the critique, adjust the corresponding forecast values by a conservative factor (reduce growth by 20-30% for that driver).
Step 2: For "Medium" risk assumptions, reduce growth by 10% for that driver.
Step 3: Leave "Low" risk assumptions unchanged.
Step 4: Calculate a confidence range: ± percentage based on the number of High (±15%) and Medium (±8%) risks.

RESPONSE FORMAT: Begin your response DIRECTLY with the opening brace { — no reasoning or text before the JSON.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
{
  "strategy": "<strategy>",
  "base_year": <YYYY>,
  "revised_forecast": [
    {"year": <YYYY>, "revenue_cr": <number>, "net_income_cr": <number>, "eps": <number>,
     "confidence_range_pct": <number>},
    ...
  ],
  "revisions_made": ["<description of each change>"],
  "final_confidence_score": <0-100>
}
